description:
FILED s533b (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-03): the half of #3801 the requalifier port does NOT reach.  PRE-EXISTING (base 4fb60297 prints the same wrong answer).

REPRODUCER (probed vs perl 5.40.3; scratch/s533b/pr/B4d.pl in the s533b worktree):

    {
        my $S = my $F = 0;
        package T;
        sub g { ++$F; ++$S }
        package main;
        T::g(); T::g();
        print "F=$F S=$S\n";
    }

perl:                    F=2 S=2
base 4fb60297 / s533b:   F=0 S=2   -- SILENT WRONG.

WHY: at FILE level a bare block with `package` statements is FLATTENED into sections (separate top-level CL forms), so a lexical used across a section boundary must be PROMOTED to a cell by the span pass (`_check_my_spanning` / `_rename_spanning_lexicals` / `_promote_captured`).  Those read a statement's declarations through `_declared_names` -> `_declarator_syms`, which returns only the symbol(s) right after the statement's FIRST keyword -- the chained `my $F` is invisible, so `$S` is promoted and `$F` is not: the main section `let`s $F and g's section reads a different symbol.  In a SUB body (no flattening) the requalifier alone decided it, and the s533b port fixed that form (#3801 sub-body form, Pl/t/requalify-binder-01.t row 3).  With a `$T::F` mention in the file the exception rename (#3080) takes `$F` instead and emits TWO `p-defcell $F__excl__0` in two packages -- still F=0 (B4c.pl).

perl-tests/local.t:649 (`my $STORE = my $FETCH = 0;` + `package TieHash; sub FETCH { ++$FETCH; 42 }` in a bare block) is this shape.

FIX: belongs to 1c port 3 (the span pass asks the binder: the ref table grouped by binding, docs/binder-dual-run-s525.md §6 item 3).  A legacy fix would widen `_declarator_syms` to every my/our/state declarator of the statement -- it has six readers (span, promote, forward-decl, the our-alias pass, `_symbol_is_declarator`), so it is an emission change to be measured, not a one-liner.
